## Question 18: Qn(u)

Let $u \in (0, 1)$. Let $n\in \mathbb N$. We have:

$$
u \leq F_n(x) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{1}_{X_i \leq x}.
$$

So, we can express:
$$
un \leq \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{1}_{X_i \leq x}.
$$

We order $(X_i)_{i=1}^n$ in ascending order to obtain a new sequence:
$$
(X_{(1)}, X_{(2)}, \dots, X_{(n)}).
$$

The quantile function $Q_n(u)$ is defined as:
$$
Q_n(u) =
\begin{cases} 
X_{(un)}, & \text{if } un \text{ is an integer,} \\
X_{\lceil un \rceil}, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

Where:
$$
\lceil un \rceil := \min \{k \in \mathbb{Z} \mid k \geq un \}.
$$

## Question 19: TCL for Qn(u)

We have:
$$
\sum_{j=1}^n Y_{j,n} = n F_n(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}).
$$

Then we have:
- Expectation:
  $$
  \mathbb{E}[Y_{j,n}] = P\left(X_j \leq Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right) = F_X\left(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right).
  $$


- Variance:
  $$
  \text{Var}(Y_{j,n}) = F_X\left(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right) \left( 1 - F_X\left(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right) \right).
  $$

- $S_n$:
  $$
  s_n = n \sum_{j=1}^n \sigma^2_{j,n} = \sqrt{n} \text{Var}(Y_{j,n}).
  $$


We have:
$$
\frac{\sum_{j=1}^n Y_{j,n} - n \mathbb{E}[Y_{j,n}]}{s_n} =
\frac{\sqrt{n} \left( F_n(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}) - F_X(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}) \right)}{\sqrt{F_X\left(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right) \left(1 - F_X\left(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right)\right)}}.
$$

Let:
$$
\sigma_{n}= \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{f(Q(u))}.
$$

We have:
$$
F_X\left(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right) = 
F_X(Q(u)) + f_X(Q(u)) \cdot t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))} + o\left(\frac{1}{n}\right), \quad \text{(applying Taylor development)}.
$$

This simplifies to:
$$
= u + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n}} + o\left(\frac{1}{n}\right).
$$

We also have:
$$
\frac{s_n}{n} = \sqrt{\frac{n(u - u^2 + O(1/\sqrt{n}))}{n^2} }= \sqrt{\frac{u - u^2}{n} + O\left(\frac{1}{n^{3/2}}\right)}.
$$

Thus, it can be rewritten as:
$$
\frac{\sum_{j=1}^n Y_{j,n} - n \mathbb{E}[Y_{j,n}]}{S_n} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^n Y_{j,n} - \mathbb{E}[Y_{j,n}]}{\frac{S_n}{n}}.
$$

Substituting:
$$
= \frac{F_n\left(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}\right) - \left(u + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n}} + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\right)\right)}{\sqrt{\frac{u - u^2}{n} + O\left(\frac{1}{n^{3/2}}\right)}}.
$$

For the numerator:
$$
F_n(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}) - u - t \sqrt{\frac{u(1-u)}{n} + O\left(\frac{1}{n}\right)} > -t \sqrt{\frac{u(1-u)}{n} + O\left(\frac{1}{n^\frac{3}{2}}\right)}.
$$

Thus:
$$
F_n(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))}) > u + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\right).
$$

We also have:
$$
Q_n(F_n(Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))})) > Q_n(u) + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\right).
$$

Expanding further:
$$
Q(u) + t \frac{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}{\sqrt{n} f(Q(u))} > Q_n(u) + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\right).
$$

Thus, we have:
$$
P\left(\sqrt{n} \frac{Q_n(u) - Q(u)}{f(Q(u)) / \sqrt{u(1-u)}} < t + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\right)\right) \xrightarrow{n \to \infty} \Phi(t),
$$
where $\Phi$ is the CDF of $N(0,1)$.

Finally, we get:
$$
P\left(\sqrt{n} \frac{Q_n(u) - Q(u)}{f(Q(u)) / \sqrt{u(1-u)}} < t\right) \xrightarrow{n \to \infty} \Phi(t),
$$
which terminates the proof.

## Question 20 Behaviour of u -> 0 or 1 

When $u \to 0$ or $1$, $Q(u) \to \infty$.

We have:
$$
\frac{f(Q(u))}{\sqrt{u(1-u)}} \xrightarrow{u \to 0 \text{ or } 1} 0.
$$

In fact, we have:
$$
\frac{f(Q(u))}{\sqrt{u(1-u)}} = \frac{1}{Q'(u) \sqrt{u(1-u)}},
$$
because:

By differentiating $F_X(Q(u)) = u$, we get:
$$
f_X(Q(u)) Q'(u) = 1.
$$

So we obtain:
$$
f_X(Q(u)) = \frac{1}{Q'(u)}.
$$

Thus, we rewrite it as:
$$
\frac{1}{\sqrt{u(1-u)} Q'(u)}.
$$

Since the numerator $\frac{1}{\sqrt{u(1-u)}}$ is integrable when $u \to 0$ or $1$, while $Q'(u)$ isn't integrable when $u \to 0$ or $1$, we conclude:
$$
\frac{1}{\sqrt{u(1-u)} Q'(u)} \xrightarrow{u \to 0 \text{ or } 1} 0.
$$


